=====================================================
PART 13 — WHICH REAL-TIME APIs ARE ACTUALLY NEEDED?
=====================================================

Recommended real-time endpoints to consider:
1. GET /api/inventory/{productId}/stream
   Purpose: push stock/availability changes to a product page. Optional; use if live stock visibility matters.

2. GET /api/seller/stream
   Purpose: push new orders and status/dashboard changes to the seller dashboard. Useful for marketplace operations.

3. Optional buyer order stream:
   GET /api/orders/{orderId}/stream
   Purpose: push order status/tracking changes to the buyer's order screen. Add only if the UI needs in-page live updates.

4. Optional admin operations stream:
   GET /api/admin/stream
   Purpose: push pending seller/product/order work to an admin dashboard. Add only if admins need live updates.

Do not create SSE endpoints for cart, checkout, payments, notifications, wishlist, every product, and every dashboard by default. Usually:
- Cart/checkout/payment: regular REST requests and responses.
- Payment result: provider webhook updates backend; frontend polls GET payment status or receives an application event if needed.
- Notifications: stored notification list + push (FCM) + unread-count API.
- Order tracking: REST refresh or optional SSE.
- Seller/admin dashboards: optional SSE.
- Live chat: WebSocket only if bidirectional chat is a product requirement.

For SSE implementation, authenticate the connection, authorize the resource, set timeouts/heartbeats, handle reconnects using event IDs where appropriate, clean up disconnected clients, and avoid leaking private information.

=====================================================
PART 14 — REMOVE, MERGE, OR DO NOT ADD
=====================================================

1. Do not add POST /api/orders as a second order creation route if POST /api/checkout already creates orders. Choose one validated flow; this roadmap chooses checkout.
2. Do not expose unrestricted POST /api/refunds. Use order-linked buyer refund requests and protected admin/provider processing.
3. Do not let buyers call reserve/release inventory freely. Make those internal checkout/order workflows, or tightly restrict service-only endpoints.
4. Do not create separate live streams for every feature. Keep only streams that have a clear UI use case.
5. Do not duplicate product endpoints indefinitely. Migrate old /api/product/add_product, /update/{id}, /delete_product/{id}, /get_product/{id}, /view_all_products toward consistent /api/products and /api/seller/products routes when ready.
6. Do not duplicate seller profile APIs and seller approval APIs. Seller self-service is under /api/seller; approval/moderation is under /api/admin/sellers.
7. Do not create buyer-facing admin endpoints or expose administrative data through normal buyer endpoints.
8. Do not add an API solely because it sounds useful. Implement optional modules only when the UI/business flow needs them.
9. Avoid hard deleting products, categories, sellers, or accounts that are referenced by orders; prefer deactivation/archiving.
10. Do not accept userId/sellerId from request body as proof of identity; derive identity from authentication and then verify ownership.

